    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Everything a treadmill draws in from the room eventually ends up in the one place you
          cannot see: between the belt and the deck. Dust, carpet fibre, hair and shed belt
          material combine there into a mildly abrasive paste, and it works against the deck on
          every rotation.
        </p>

        <div id="why-it-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Why This Matters More Than It Sounds</h2>
<p>
            Lubrication gets all the attention and cleaning is the step that makes it work. The two
            are not alternatives; applying fresh silicone over accumulated grit traps the abrasive
            directly against the deck surface, which is precisely the friction you were trying to
            remove.
          </p>
          <p>
            The material that accumulates is worse than ordinary dust. Carpet fibre is the main
            offender in most homes and it is drawn in continuously by the motor's cooling fan. Belt
            backing sheds fine particles as it wears. Add hair, skin and general household dust and
            the result is a fibrous mat that holds grit against the deck and resists being pushed
            aside by the belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            The consequence is the familiar chain: friction rises, the motor draws more current to
            hold belt speed, and sustained current draw wears out motors and controller boards. So
            this twenty-minute job is not housekeeping. It is one of the few things that directly
            addresses the failure that ends most treadmills.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-you-need" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What You Will Need</h2>
<p>
            Nothing specialised, and most of it is already in the house.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The Allen key that came with the machine</strong>, for the rear roller
            adjustment bolts. If it is lost, the size is in the manual and a standard set will
            cover it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A vacuum with a narrow crevice tool</strong> and, ideally, a soft brush
            attachment.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lint-free microfibre cloths</strong> — several. Not paper towel and not an old
            T-shirt, both of which shed fibre exactly where you are trying to remove it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Isopropyl alcohol</strong>, for the deck and belt underside. It evaporates
            cleanly and leaves no residue, which household cleaners do not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>100% silicone treadmill lubricant</strong>, for afterwards — assuming your deck
            is not pre-waxed. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-lubricant/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubricant guide</a>
            covers which product and how to tell which deck you have.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Step One: Unplug and Slacken the Belt</h2>
<p>
            Unplug at the wall. Then find the two rear roller adjustment bolts, one at each back
            corner of the frame, usually behind a small cap or accessible through a hole in the end
            cap.
          </p>
          <p>
            Turn each <strong>counter-clockwise by one full turn</strong>, and keep them even —
            count the turns aloud if it helps, because uneven adjustment is what pulls the belt off
            centre later. One turn each is usually enough to lift the belt edge comfortably; two if
            the belt is tight.
          </p>
          <p>
            Note exactly how many turns you made. You will reverse this at the end, and starting
            from a known position is much easier than guessing at correct tension from scratch.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the machine folds, leave it flat for this job. Working on a folded deck puts the
            belt at an awkward angle and makes it harder to reach the centre, which is where most
            of the debris is.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-two" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Step Two: Clear the Loose Debris</h2>
<p>
            Before any cloth or liquid, get the loose material out. Lift the belt edge on one side
            and run the vacuum's crevice tool as far under as it will comfortably reach, working
            along the full length of the deck. Then do the other side.
          </p>
          <p>
            Expect to be surprised the first time. On a machine that has been on carpet for a
            couple of years without this being done, there is often a genuine mat of fibre along
            the deck edges.
          </p>
          <p>
            Also vacuum the deck edges either side of the belt, the gap between the deck and the
            side rails, and the area around the rear roller. Debris migrates to the rear and packs
            around the roller ends, where it can interfere with tracking.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do not use compressed air. It drives debris into the motor compartment and into the
            roller bearings — exactly the two places you least want it — rather than removing it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-three" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Step Three: Clean the Deck Surface</h2>
<p>
            Dampen a lint-free cloth with isopropyl alcohol. Damp, not wet — you want no liquid
            running anywhere.
          </p>
          <p>
            Lift the belt edge and work the cloth under, wiping <strong>from the centre of the deck
            outward</strong> rather than back and forth. Back-and-forth motion redistributes what
            you are trying to remove; working outward carries it to the edge where you can lift it
            off.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Change to a clean cloth face frequently.</strong> This is the step people
            shortcut, and a saturated cloth simply spreads residue in a thinner layer. You should
            be able to see the material transferring to the cloth; when a face stops picking
            anything up, turn it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Work from both sides so you cover the full width including the centre, which is where
            almost all the belt contact happens and therefore where cleaning matters most. Keep
            liquid well away from the front of the machine, where the motor and controller board
            are.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-four" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Step Four: Clean the Belt Underside</h2>
<p>
            Half the interface is the belt, and it is routinely ignored. The fabric backing holds
            residue as readily as the deck does.
          </p>
          <p>
            With the belt slack, lift a section and wipe the underside with a fresh alcohol-damped
            cloth. Then rotate the belt by hand — push it along by the top surface — to bring the
            next section into reach, and repeat until you have been all the way round.
          </p>
          <p>
            This takes longer than the deck and is worth doing properly. A clean deck under a dirty
            belt is a clean deck for about ten minutes.
          </p>
          <p>
            While the belt is slack and you are turning it, take the opportunity to look at it: the
            edges for fraying, the backing for wear, and the layers for any sign of separation.
            Early delamination is much easier to act on than late, and this is the one time a year
            you will have this view.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-five" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Step Five: Let It Dry Completely</h2>
<p>
            Isopropyl alcohol evaporates quickly, but "quickly" is not "instantly", and applying
            lubricant onto a surface that is still damp gives you a diluted film that does not
            perform.
          </p>
          <p>
            Give it <strong>fifteen to thirty minutes</strong> with the belt slack so air can reach
            both surfaces. If the room is cold or humid, allow longer. There is no benefit to
            rushing this and a real cost to getting it wrong.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is a convenient point to do the other annual tasks while you have access. Turn
            each roller by hand and feel for roughness or catching — smooth and silent is correct,
            gritty means bearings are wearing. Look at the deck surface for polishing or grooves in
            the belt path, which indicates the low-friction layer is wearing through and the deck
            may be due for flipping or replacement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-six" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Step Six: Lubricate and Retension</h2>
<p>
            With everything clean and dry, this is the ideal moment to lubricate — the whole point
            of cleaning first.
          </p>
          <p>
            Apply <strong>about an ounce of 100% silicone</strong> along the centre third of the
            deck under the belt, using an applicator bottle rather than an aerosol. Check your
            manual first: pre-waxed decks must never receive silicone. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication guide</a>
            covers the procedure in full.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then <strong>retension</strong>: turn each rear roller bolt clockwise by exactly the
            number of turns you loosened. Check with the lift test — the belt should rise about two
            to three inches at the centre of the deck. Our
            <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">tension guide</a>
            covers it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Plug in, run at a walking pace unloaded for three to five minutes to distribute the
            lubricant, and <strong>watch the tracking</strong>. Slackening and retensioning is the
            most likely moment for the belt to start drifting, and our
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">centring guide</a>
            covers the quarter-turn correction.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-often" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. How Often to Do This</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Annually</strong> for a machine in a normal heated room on a hard floor with
            average use. That is the baseline, and for many owners it is enough.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Every six months</strong> if the machine sits on carpet, lives in a garage or
            basement, is used by two or more people daily, or shares a home with a shedding pet.
            All four increase the rate at which material arrives under the belt, and carpet is by
            far the largest factor.
          </p>
          <p>
            You do not need the full procedure at every lubrication. The routine 40-to-50-hour
            lubrication includes a wipe of the deck and belt underside, which handles ordinary
            accumulation. This deeper clean — belt fully slackened, vacuumed, both surfaces done
            end to end — is the annual version of that. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance checklist</a>
            sets out which tasks belong to which interval.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-you-find" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. What You Find, and What It Tells You</h2>
<p>
            The state of things under the belt is diagnostic, so it is worth looking rather than
            just cleaning.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Fine grey dust, evenly spread.</strong> Normal. This is ordinary household dust
            plus a little belt shedding, and it means the interval you are on is about right.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Matted fibre along the deck edges.</strong> Carpet, almost always. Consider a
            rigid mat under the machine and shorten this interval to six months. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">carpet guide</a>
            covers why this matters for the motor as well as the deck.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A dark, sticky film.</strong> Old lubricant that has picked up debris, or the
            residue of an inappropriate product. Clean it thoroughly — this is exactly the abrasive
            layer worth removing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A shiny, polished or grooved deck surface.</strong> The low-friction layer is
            wearing through. Many decks are reversible, and flipping one effectively doubles deck
            life. Worth investigating before the wear becomes a motor problem.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Black powder concentrated near one roller.</strong> Belt backing wearing
            unevenly, usually a tracking problem that has been running for a while. Correct the
            tracking and check the belt for damage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. The Short Version</h2>
<p>
            Unplug the machine. Loosen both rear roller bolts one full turn each, counting. Vacuum
            under the belt from both sides. Wipe the deck with a lint-free cloth damped in
            isopropyl alcohol, working centre-outward and changing cloth faces often. Wipe the belt
            underside, rotating the belt by hand to reach it all.
          </p>
          <p>
            Let it dry fifteen to thirty minutes. Lubricate with 100% silicone — after checking
            your manual for deck type. Retension by the same number of turns, confirm with the lift
            test, then run it slowly and watch the tracking.
          </p>
          <p>
            Twenty minutes, once a year, six months if the machine is on carpet. It is the step
            that makes lubrication work rather than an alternative to it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you clean under a treadmill belt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Unplug the machine, loosen both rear roller bolts one full turn each, and vacuum under the belt from both sides with a crevice tool. Then wipe the deck with a lint-free cloth damped in isopropyl alcohol, working centre-outward and changing cloth faces often, and do the belt underside the same way.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you clean under a treadmill belt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Annually for a machine in a normal heated room on a hard floor. Every six months if it sits on carpet, lives in a garage or basement, is used by two or more people daily, or shares a home with a shedding pet. Carpet is by far the largest single factor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I use to clean a treadmill deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Isopropyl alcohol on a lint-free microfibre cloth, damp rather than wet. It evaporates cleanly and leaves no residue. Avoid household cleaners, which leave films, and avoid paper towel or old T-shirts, which shed fibre exactly where you are trying to remove it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use compressed air to clean under a treadmill belt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Better not to. Compressed air drives debris into the motor compartment and into the roller bearings rather than removing it, which are the two places you least want it. A vacuum with a narrow crevice tool takes material out instead of relocating it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to lubricate after cleaning under the belt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is the ideal moment — a clean, dry deck is exactly what fresh lubricant needs. Apply about an ounce of 100% silicone along the centre third under the belt, then retension and run the machine slowly for a few minutes. Check your manual first, as pre-waxed decks must never receive silicone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is there black powder under my treadmill belt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Belt backing wearing away. A light, even distribution is normal shedding. If it is concentrated near one roller, the belt has probably been tracking off-centre for a while and rubbing — correct the tracking and inspect the belt edge for damage before it worsens.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Cleaning Under a Treadmill Belt</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The first two undo the work entirely, and the fourth is the one that turns a clean into a tracking problem.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Lubricating without cleaning first</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Fresh silicone applied over accumulated grit traps the abrasive directly against the deck, which is the friction you were trying to remove. Cleaning is not an optional extra step before lubrication — it is what makes lubrication work. Wipe the deck and belt underside and let both dry fully first.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using paper towel or an old T-shirt</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Both shed fibre, and you are depositing it in exactly the place you are trying to clear. Use lint-free microfibre, and change to a clean face frequently — a saturated cloth spreads residue in a thinner layer rather than removing it. You should see material transferring to the cloth.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blowing debris out with compressed air</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">It drives fibre and grit into the motor compartment and the roller bearings instead of removing it. Those are the two places where contamination causes the most expensive problems. Use a vacuum with a narrow crevice tool, which takes the material away.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not counting the turns when slackening the belt</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Loosening the rear roller bolts by an unknown, possibly uneven amount means guessing at correct tension afterwards, and uneven tension is what pulls a belt off centre. Count the turns aloud, keep both sides equal, and reverse exactly at the end — then confirm with the lift test.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Related Guides -->
